<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.6.3: Connecting Graphical and Verbal Representations of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00945</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.6.3: Connecting Graphical and Verbal Representations of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7585884a-37f5-4d07-b422-092ff567a6fd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">In a bungee jump, the height of the jumper is a function of time since the jump begins.</para>
        <!-- Graph Cell -->
          <para id="para-00002">
                  <media alt="Graph of curve. Horizontal axis, 0 to 35 by 5’s, t, seconds. Vertical axis, 0 to 90 by 10’s, h, meters. Curve starts a 0 comma 80, decreases then increases, 4 times.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_06_01.svg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
        
        <!-- Expressions and Equations Cell -->
          <para id="para-00003">Expressions and Equations:</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></item>
            <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
            <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></item>
            <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>80</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
            <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>45</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
          </list>
        
        <!-- Features Cell -->
          <para id="para-00004">Features:</para>
          <list id="list-00002">
            <item>first dip in the graph</item>
            <item>vertical intercept</item>
            <item>first peak in the graph</item>
            <item>horizontal intercept</item>
            <item>maximum</item>
          </list>
        
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
        <item>Match each description about the jump to a corresponding expression or equation and to a feature on the graph.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00005">One expression or equation does not have a matching verbal description. Its corresponding graphical feature is also not shown on the graph. Interpret that expression or equation in terms of the jump and in terms of the graph of the function. Record your interpretation in the last row of the table.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
              <para id="para-00006"><emphasis>Description of Jump</emphasis></para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Expression or Equation</emphasis></para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Feature of Graph</emphasis></para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00004">
                <item>the greatest height that the jumper is from the river</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00005">
                <item>the height from which the jumper was jumping</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00006">
                <item>the time at which the jumper reached the highest point after the first bounce</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00007">
                <item>the lowest point that the jumper reached in the entire jump</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00008">
                <item>the time at which the jumper hits the surface of the river</item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
            <entry> </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00009">Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>80</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00012">Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">maximum</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00015">Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00018">Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00020">vertical intercept</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00023"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>45</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00024">Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00026">first peak in the graph</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00027">Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00029"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00030">Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00032">first dip in the graph; also the minimum of the graph shown</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00033">Description of Jump</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00035">the time at which the jumper hits the surface of the river</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00036">Expression or Equation</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00037">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00038"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00011">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00039">Feature of Graph</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00011">
          <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00041">horizontal intercept</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00042">2. Use the graph to:</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00012">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00043">estimate <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00012">
          <para id="para-00044">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00045"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is 80, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is about 10.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- Question 2 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00013">
        <problem id="prob-00013">
          <para id="para-00046">If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> equals what value?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00013">
          <para id="para-00047">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00048"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>≈</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- Question 3 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00014">
        <problem id="prob-00014">
          <para id="para-00049">If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>45</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> equals what value?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00014">
          <para id="para-00050">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00051">The approximate solutions to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>45</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> are <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- Question 4 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00015">
        <problem id="prob-00015">
          <para id="para-00052">If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> equals what value?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00015">
          <para id="para-00053">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00054">There is no solution to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is between 0 and 35, which is what is shown on the graph. We don't know if the function will have a solution when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is greater than 35.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End --></section>
      <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: The Meaning of Function Notation</title>
      <para id="para-00055">Watch the following video to learn more about connecting graphical and verbal representations of a function.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/08336f60e67c7d416294bbeff0c6da750df4db0f"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00056">The horizontal intercept is also called the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-<term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">intercept,</term> or <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">zero</term>.</para>
      <para id="para-00057">The vertical intercept is also called the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math><term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">-intercept</term>.</para></section>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
          <section id="sect-00004">
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00016">
        <problem id="prob-00016">
          <para id="para-00058">Based on the information available, how long do you think the bungee cord is? Make an estimate and explain your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00016">
          <para id="para-00059">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00060">About 50 meters. The low points on the graph increase with each bounce, and the high points decrease. They look like they are approaching about 30 meters in height above the river. Since the jump started from 80 meters above the river, this means that the bungee cord is about 50 meters long.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section></section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00061">The graph models the height, in feet, of a kickball over time. When is the ball at its maximum height?</para>
      <para id="para-00062">
          <media alt="GRAPH THAT SHOWS THE HEIGHT OF A KICKBALL IN FEET AS A FUNCTION OF TIME. THE FUNCTION IS MODELED BY AN UPSIDE DOWN PARABOLA WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF 0, \(x\)-intercepts OF 0 AND 5, AND MAXIMUM AT THE POINT (2.5, 31).  ">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_07_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section id="sect-00006">
      <title>More Real-Life Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00063">The graph below represents the total number of smartphones that are shipped to a retail store over the course of 50 days.</para>
      <para id="para-00064">
          <media alt="GRAPH THAT SHOWS THE NUMBER OF SMARTPHONES SHIPPED TO A RETAIL STORE AS A FUNCTION OF TIME IN DAYS. THE X-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 50 IN INTERVALS OF 10. THE Y-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 4000 IN INCREMENTS OF 1000. THE GRAPH IS INCREASING LINEARLY FROM X = 0 TO X = 20, REMAINS CONSTANT FROM X = 20 TO X = 22, AND INCREASES FROM X = 22 TO X = 50.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_08_01.jpg" width="300"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00065">Why does <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>22</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
      <para id="para-00066">Since section B is not changing, it represents the period of time where the store did not receive new shipments, so <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>22</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
  <title>Try It: More Real-Life Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00067">The relationship between Jameson’s account balance <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>B</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in dollars, and time <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in days, is modeled by the graph below.</para>
      <para id="para-00068">
          <media alt="GRAPH THAT SHOWS ACCOUNT BALANCE IN DOLLARS AS A FUNCTION OF TIME IN DAYS. THE X-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 14 IN INTERVALS OF 2. THE Y-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 100 IN INTERVALS OF 20. THE GRAPH IS CONSTANT FROM X = 0 TO X = 6, INCREASES FROM X = 6 TO X = 9, REACHING A MAXIMUM AT THE POINT (9, 100), AND THEN DECREASES.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_08_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00069">What does the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>B</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>98</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represent in the context of the problem?</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00017">
          <problem id="prob-00017">
            <para id="para-00070">Write your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
  </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00017">
            <para id="para-00071">Here is how to describe what <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>B</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>98</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents in the context of the problem:</para>
            <para id="para-00072"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>B</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>98</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> represents the maximum amount of money Jameson had in his account over the time period shown. It also represents that after 9 days, Jameson had $98 in his account. It is the maximum because it is the highest point on the graph provided.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    
  </content>
</document>